{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["<b>I call to witness against you this day the heavens and the earth.</b> I have already mentioned in the perashah of כי תוליד (Deuteronomy 4:26) the explanation of the testimony of the heavens and the earth — that they are eternal witnesses, for all generations pass away while they endure. Furthermore, it may include the angels in heaven and human beings on earth, and it may also include the rain and the fruits. After Moses finished his reproofs and words of prophecy, he summoned these witnesses, like one who has the witnesses sign at the end of his words." "<b>It is also possible</b> to explain further regarding the testimony of the heavens and the earth, that it alludes to their endurance after the destruction: for on the seventh day there will be destruction of all human and beast, yet the heavens and the earth will endure. Therefore, after Moses mentioned the matter of redemption and attached to it the resurrection of the dead, as it says, ״ראה נתתי לפניך היום את החיים״ — and he attached to the resurrection of the dead the World to Come, which is the good — he needed to summon witnesses that endure, which are what remain in the world, as it says (Isaiah 2:11–12): ״ונשגב ה׳ לבדו ביום ההוא״, for they correspond to the heavens and the earth in the first verse. And understand this." "<b>And in the Midrash:</b> ״I call to witness against you this day the heavens and the earth״ — the Holy One, blessed be He, said to Israel: Look at the heavens that I created to serve you — perhaps they changed their measure? Perhaps the wheel of the sun did not rise from the east and illuminate the whole world, in the manner of what is said (Ecclesiastes 1:5): ״וזרח השמש ובא השמש״? Look at the earth that I created to serve you — perhaps it changed its measure? Perhaps you sowed it with wheat and it did not grow, or perhaps you sowed it with wheat and it produced barley? Now these, which were not made either for reward or for punishment — for if they merit they do not receive reward, and if they sin they do not receive punishment — did not change their measure. You, however, who if you merit will receive reward and if you sin will receive punishment, how much more so! Therefore: ״ובחרת בחיים״ — like a man who says to his fellow, ״A fine portion in my inheritance is set aside for you,״ and he stands him before the good and says to him, ״This is set aside for you.״ So explained Rashi, of blessed memory. And this is what is written (Psalms 16:5): ״ה׳ מנת חלקי וכוסי אתה תומיך גורלי״. David said before the Holy One, blessed be He: Master of the universe, place my hand upon the good lot, for the Holy One, blessed be He, is called ״good,״ as it says (Psalms 145:9): ״טוב ה׳ לכל״, (ibid. 25:8): ״טוב וישר ה׳״. And He gave Israel the Torah, which is called ״a good teaching,״ as it says (Proverbs 4:2): ״כי לקח טוב נתתי לכם״. And with it you will have in this world the life and the good, as it says: ״ראה נתתי לפניך היום את החיים ואת הטוב״. And they will merit the World to Come, all of which is good, as our Rabbis, of blessed memory, expounded (Deuteronomy 5:16): ״למען יאריכון ימיך״ — to the world all of which is long; ״ולמען ייטב לך״ — to the world all of which is good. And so Solomon, peace be upon him, said (Proverbs 24:25): ״ולמוכיחים ינעם ועליהם תבא ברכת טוב״."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 30, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:52:36.607215676Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}